9
00:00:19,545 --> 00:00:20,774
Question 1 of 3.

10
00:00:21,967 --> 00:00:23,907
A new screening test for a disease has

11
00:00:23,927 --> 00:00:27,723
a sensitivity of 95% and a specificity of

12
00:00:27,743 --> 00:00:31,199
90%. The disease has a prevalence of 1%

13
00:00:31,219 --> 00:00:33,027
in the population being screened.

14
00:00:34,100 --> 00:00:35,868
Here is the finding that decides it.

15
00:00:36,103 --> 00:00:37,858
Prevalence: 1%

16
00:00:38,560 --> 00:00:41,355
If a person from this population tests positive,

17
00:00:41,599 --> 00:00:43,658
what is the probability that they actually have

18
00:00:43,678 --> 00:00:44,539
the disease?

19
00:00:45,226 --> 00:00:47,682
Pause here. Commit to an answer before the

20
00:00:47,702 --> 00:00:48,629
explanation.

21
00:00:53,193 --> 00:00:54,593
The answer is A.

22
00:00:54,904 --> 00:00:56,172
8.8%.

23
00:00:56,863 --> 00:01:01,727
Option A. 8.8%. In 10,000 people, 100 have

24
00:01:01,747 --> 00:01:04,555
the disease and 95 of those test positive;

25
00:01:04,734 --> 00:01:09,214
of the 9,900 without disease, 990 test positive

26
00:01:09,234 --> 00:01:13,963
falsely. Of 1,085 positives only 95 are true,

27
00:01:14,208 --> 00:01:19,081
giving 95/1,085 = 8.8%.

28
00:01:20,163 --> 00:01:24,632
Option B. 95%. This is the sensitivity, which

29
00:01:24,652 --> 00:01:27,290
answers the opposite question: among people who

30
00:01:27,310 --> 00:01:30,066
have the disease, what fraction test positive.

31
00:01:30,297 --> 00:01:32,040
For B to be the answer, you would

32
00:01:32,060 --> 00:01:34,817
need this: A question asking for the proportion

33
00:01:34,837 --> 00:01:37,475
of diseased patients who test positive, not the

34
00:01:37,495 --> 00:01:39,842
proportion of positives who are diseased.

35
00:01:40,930 --> 00:01:45,320
Option C. 90%. This is the specificity: among

36
00:01:45,340 --> 00:01:47,504
people who are well, what fraction test

37
00:01:47,524 --> 00:01:50,281
negative. It ignores prevalence and runs in the

38
00:01:50,301 --> 00:01:52,951
wrong direction. For C to be the answer,

39
00:01:53,051 --> 00:01:55,347
you would need this: A question asking what

40
00:01:55,367 --> 00:01:57,635
fraction of healthy people test negative.

41
00:01:58,663 --> 00:02:03,698
Option D. 50%. A 50% positive predictive value

42
00:02:03,718 --> 00:02:06,132
would require prevalence high enough to balance

43
00:02:06,152 --> 00:02:07,645
the false positive rate.

44
00:02:07,876 --> 00:02:10,817
At 1% prevalence the false positives swamp the

45
00:02:10,837 --> 00:02:13,592
true positives. For D to be the answer,

46
00:02:13,745 --> 00:02:16,238
you would need this: A prevalence roughly equal

47
00:02:16,258 --> 00:02:18,580
to the false positive rate, which at these

48
00:02:18,600 --> 00:02:21,158
test characteristics is about 10%.

49
00:02:22,163 --> 00:02:26,527
Option E. 0.9%. This is the false positive

50
00:02:26,547 --> 00:02:29,014
rate applied to the whole population, not a

51
00:02:29,034 --> 00:02:31,711
predictive value. It answers no question that

52
00:02:31,731 --> 00:02:34,079
was asked. For E to be the answer,

53
00:02:34,205 --> 00:02:36,422
you would need this: A question asking for

54
00:02:36,442 --> 00:02:38,672
the probability that a healthy person tests

55
00:02:38,692 --> 00:02:43,000
positive, which is 10% here, not 0.9%.

56
00:02:44,063 --> 00:02:47,016
Sensitivity and specificity belong to the test.

57
00:02:47,250 --> 00:02:49,704
Predictive values belong to the patient in front

58
00:02:49,724 --> 00:02:51,874
of you, and they move with prevalence.

59
00:02:52,105 --> 00:02:54,717
Never answer a predictive-value question before

60
00:02:54,737 --> 00:02:56,255
writing down the prevalence.

61
