76
00:03:43,691 --> 00:03:45,459
Here is the finding that decides it.

77
00:03:45,693 --> 00:03:48,172
Naloxone is a competitive antagonist

78
00:03:48,870 --> 00:03:51,455
How does naloxone produce rapid improvement in

79
00:03:51,475 --> 00:03:53,678
opioid-induced respiratory depression?

80
00:03:54,398 --> 00:03:56,854
Pause here. Commit to an answer before the

81
00:03:56,874 --> 00:03:57,800
explanation.

82
00:04:02,365 --> 00:04:03,673
The answer is A.

83
00:04:03,720 --> 00:04:06,515
Competitive blockade at opioid receptors.

84
00:04:07,219 --> 00:04:10,041
Option A. Competitive blockade at opioid

85
00:04:10,061 --> 00:04:13,741
receptors. Naloxone rapidly displaces opioids at

86
00:04:13,761 --> 00:04:16,412
μ-opioid receptors because it is a competitive

87
00:04:16,432 --> 00:04:19,517
antagonist. This restores normal respiratory

88
00:04:19,537 --> 00:04:22,096
drive quickly when the receptor is no longer

89
00:04:22,116 --> 00:04:23,871
occupied by the agonist.

90
00:04:24,919 --> 00:04:28,096
Option B. Irreversible destruction of opioid

91
00:04:28,116 --> 00:04:31,125
receptors. Irreversible receptor damage would

92
00:04:31,145 --> 00:04:33,769
not explain rapid recovery within minutes.

93
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:36,678
It would be unlikely to show quick, reversible

94
00:04:36,698 --> 00:04:39,821
physiologic improvement. For B to be the answer,

95
00:04:40,013 --> 00:04:42,033
you would need this: Change the vignette to

96
00:04:42,053 --> 00:04:44,533
show that recovery takes days and includes

97
00:04:44,553 --> 00:04:47,717
evidence of receptor loss (eg, no response to

98
00:04:47,737 --> 00:04:50,519
further naloxone), which is not present here.

99
00:04:51,619 --> 00:04:55,336
Option C. Inhibition of hepatic CYP450

100
00:04:55,356 --> 00:04:58,772
metabolism of opioids. Blocking metabolism would

101
00:04:58,792 --> 00:05:01,364
prolong opioid effects rather than rapidly

102
00:05:01,384 --> 00:05:04,022
reverse them. The expected outcome would be

103
00:05:04,042 --> 00:05:06,878
persistent hypoventilation. For C to be the

104
00:05:06,898 --> 00:05:09,430
answer, you would need this: Alter the vignette

105
00:05:09,450 --> 00:05:12,101
so naloxone worsens or does not improve

106
00:05:12,121 --> 00:05:14,062
respiratory depression; that would fit

107
00:05:14,082 --> 00:05:17,403
metabolism inhibition, not receptor antagonism.

108
00:05:18,585 --> 00:05:21,946
Option D. Opening of GABA-A chloride channels.

109
00:05:22,180 --> 00:05:24,765
GABA-A activation causes sedation and

110
00:05:24,785 --> 00:05:27,213
respiratory depression, not reversal of

111
00:05:27,233 --> 00:05:30,134
opioid-induced hypoventilation. It would worsen

112
00:05:30,154 --> 00:05:32,883
the presentation. For D to be the answer,

113
00:05:33,022 --> 00:05:35,568
you would need this: Modify the vignette so

114
00:05:35,588 --> 00:05:37,660
the patient becomes more somnolent and

115
00:05:37,680 --> 00:05:40,093
hypoventilates after the reversal agent,

116
00:05:40,259 --> 00:05:42,435
consistent with GABA-A potentiation.

117
00:05:43,619 --> 00:05:46,599
Option E. Activation of cannabinoid receptors

118
00:05:46,619 --> 00:05:48,571
leading to respiratory stimulation.

119
00:05:48,806 --> 00:05:51,259
Cannabinoid receptor activation is not the

120
00:05:51,279 --> 00:05:54,233
mechanism of naloxone. In this vignette, the

121
00:05:54,253 --> 00:05:57,101
classic opioid toxidrome is present and is

122
00:05:57,121 --> 00:06:00,140
specifically reversed by opioid antagonism.

123
00:06:00,371 --> 00:06:01,996
For E to be the answer, you would

124
00:06:02,016 --> 00:06:04,036
need this: Change the vignette hook to

125
00:06:04,056 --> 00:06:06,522
explicitly describe synthetic cannabinoid

126
00:06:06,542 --> 00:06:09,312
intoxication without opioid signs; then a

127
00:06:09,332 --> 00:06:11,745
